      "judged_assistant_text": {
        "text": "**A $10,000 holding could be worth roughly:**\n\n| Scenario | Ten-year value range |\n|---|---:|\n| Bear | **$32,800, $109,000** |\n| Base | **$77,500, $610,400** |\n| Bull | **$163,800, $2.72 million** |\n\nThese are very wide scenarios, not predictions. A current September market price could not be retrieved from Grayscale\u2019s page, so the calculation is anchored to its official **January 9, 2026** figures: Bitcoin index price **$90,198.49**, ETF market price **$39.94**, and annual sponsor fee **0.15%**.\n\nThe nearest published forecast horizon is 2030. ARK\u2019s published model used Bitcoin targets of approximately **$300,000**, **$710,000**, and **$1.5 million** for its bear, base, and bull cases.\n\nFor each ten-year range, I used two post-2030 assumptions:\n\n- **Low end:** Bitcoin reaches the relevant 2030 target and then remains at that price through the end of the holding period.\n- **High end:** Bitcoin\u2019s first five-year price multiplier repeats during the following five years.\n\nThe arithmetic is:\n\n- **Low end:** $10,000 \u00d7 (2030 target \u00f7 $90,198.49) \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.0015)^10.\n- **High end:** $10,000 \u00d7 (2030 target \u00f7 $90,198.49)^2 \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.0015)^10.\n\nThe ETF is passively invested in Bitcoin and seeks to reflect the value of its Bitcoin holdings after expenses and liabilities, so these calculations apply Bitcoin-price scenarios and the stated sponsor fee. They exclude taxes, trading costs, premium or discount changes, and tracking differences. No investment advice.",
